{
  "text": "2020A01 Outline the possible reasons for prolonged\nparalysis induced by an intravenous dose of 1 mg/kg of suxamethonium.\nList:\n·\nIntro\n·\nEnzyme abnormality (sux apnoea)\n·\nEnzyme deficiency (phys, path)\n·\nEnzyme inhibition (drug)\nIntro:\n·\nUsual dose 1mg/kg\n·\nUsual offset 5-10 mins due to high capacity butyrylchoineterase\n(BChE)\n·\nTwo stage metabolism to choline and succinic\nacid\nBChE abnormality\n(sux apnoea):\nGenetics\n·\nTwo copies\n·\nFour alleles:\no\nNormal (N)\no\nDibucaine-resistance (DR)\no\nChloride-resistant (CR)\no\nAbsent (A)\nAlleles\n% population\nDibucaine number\nDuration\nN-N\n96%\n80\n5-10mins\nN-DR\n<4%\n60\n15-30mins\nDR-DR\n0.03%\n20\n8-12 hours\nTest\n·\nPatient’s plasma + dibucaine + 10\n-5\nbenzylcholine\n·\nDibucaine number = % inhibition of benzylcholine\n  metabolism\n·\nNormal-normal -> 80% inhibition (as above)\nManagement\n·\nIf no reversal after 30 mins\n·\nProlonged ventilation and sedation in ICU\n·\nMonitor with nerve stimulator\n·\n+/- FFP\nBChE deficiency\n(rarely significant):\nPhysiological causes\n·\nNeonate -> ↓production\n·\nPregnancy -> ↑ECF water -> same amount\n  but ↓concentration\nPathological causes\n·\nHeart failure\n·\nLiver failure\n·\nRenal failure\n·\nThyrotoxicosis\n·\nCancer\nBChE inhibition\n(rarely significant):\n·\nAcetylcholinesterase inhibitors e.g. neostigmine\n·\nMetoclopramide\n·\nKetamine\n·\nOral contraceptive\n·\nLithium\n·\nLignocaine\n·\nEster local anaesthetics\n·\nCyclophosphamide\nFeedback welcome\nat\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
